promised to protect our environment  we're getting it done the government has  made bold statements on climate change  hence the expectation this budget would  put significant building blocks in place  to deliver on its promise to make  Britain carbon neutral by 2050 instead  environmentalists say there were some  eye-catching measures but not enough to  make the transition towards a low-carbon  economy in his budget statement the  Chancellor announced plans for a plastic  packaging tax there's to be a tree  planting project to create a forest  bigger than Birmingham and funding for  flood defences will be doubled but other  measures will do little to tackle  climate change fuel duty has been frozen  for the tenth consecutive year 27  billion pounds will be spent on Road  building and overall there were no  significant announcements on large-scale  green infrastructure I think this budget  may have caused people to sober up a  little bit and think twice about whether  the government's rhetoric actually  matches its priorities but you know  let's let's wait and see what's actually  planned in the infrastructure  assessments in the next couple of months  but slightly on the face of it he didn't  flag anything that sounded particularly  green there was little in the budget to  help homeowners adjust towards a  low-carbon future on transport the  sector with the highest emissions there  were no major announcements on railway  improvements in the end the focus of  this budget was on the economy and of  course tackling coronavirus but there  was little sign of the leadership on  climate change which the government has  repeatedly promised Lisa Holland Sky  News
 

